From: sie-mcp web <web@sie-mcp.local>
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] wasm: Asyncify-safe futex/condvar + skip the
 per-insn icount2 helper

Under Asyncify, emscripten's pthread_cond_signal/broadcast between web
workers is only delivered when the waiter times out, so QemuCond used
for cross-thread wakeup (the pmb887x DSP worker boot-command handshake)
sleeps forever: the L1 task times out and the phone never draws its
splash screen.  The engine-level futex (Atomics.wait/notify) is
Asyncify-safe.

- include/qemu/futex.h: implement the qemu_futex wrappers on
  emscripten_futex_wait/wake; HAVE_FUTEX now defined on emscripten,
  switching QemuEvent/LockCnt to their futex fast paths.
- util/qemu-thread-posix.c + include/qemu/thread-posix.h: futex/seq
  based QemuCond for emscripten (pthread-based everywhere else).
  qemu_cond_timedwait now reports timeouts (emscripten_futex_wait
  returns -ETIMEDOUT): qemu_sem_timedwait and the thread-pool idle
  exit actually time out on wasm, like on POSIX.

Additionally, TCI helper calls route through libffi, which on wasm is a
JS roundtrip; the per-guest-instruction icount2 cycle helper capped the
interpreter at ~0.6M insns/s.  On emscripten, skip the per-instruction
helper (target/arm/tcg/translate.c) — the per-TB accounting that
replaces it is the tci_tbhdr header op of patch 0007.  icount2's 1 MHz
frequency floor is likewise relaxed on emscripten only (the TCI rate on
wasm is far below it; the controller converges to the real rate).

Wasm-only by nature (Asyncify/emscripten does not exist elsewhere);
icount2 parts are fork-specific either way.  config-host bits:
-sASYNCIFY_REMOVE=tcg_qemu_tb_exec keeps the interpreter itself out of
the Asyncify instrumentation (reachable via libffi's ffi_call_js import,
never suspends itself; ~2x interpreter speed).
---
